00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:02.990 align:start position:0%
as with other first-person adventure  games like the seminal gone home  Firewatch isn't about combat or even  puzzles instead it's about atmosphere  and exploration both of an expansive  wyoming forest and a burgeoning  friendship the story follows Henry a  middle-aged fire lookout working near  Yellowstone National Park in the summer  of 1989 the painterly art style infused  the wilderness with a welcoming warmth  and the ever-present sounds of nature  make every moment spent in Firewatch  feel like all the best parts of a  real-life camping trip you spend the  vast majority of the game wandering  alone through fire watches dense  detailed forests but the game's real  focus rests on Henry's relationship with  his boss Delilah which manifests as an  extensive series of walkie-talkie  conversations with branching dialogue  options their humor and vulnerability  make both characters not only memorable  but sympathetic which makes shaping the  growth and evolution of their  relationship a truly unique joy  unfortunately the central mystery can't  quite match the quality of the  characters at first I got wrapped up in  the panic and paranoia but once I  learned the truth several story moments  simply didn't add up  it's a shame fire watch fails as a  mystery because it succeeds in so many  other ways its world is captivating its  design is clever and its characters are  beautifully human  [Music]
 

